    <estimated-effort>8-12 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Session 27 C10 research gap 5: "Internal sharees of the parent lose sight of re-owned children unless shares cascade or the one-hop inheritance (tasks 055/056) is built." Owner round 2 item 6 puts it in scope.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Once task 146 makes every child of a secure record owned by the memberless Secure owner team, the only people who can read those children are people with an explicit share on each child row. The people shared on the PARENT must see its children — no one else. First find out, live, whether Dataverse's relationship cascade already does this for new and existing children; if it does not cover every case, make the BFF mirror the parent's share set onto its children and keep it mirrored.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer on Dataverse row security (principalobjectaccess, relationship cascade configuration) and the BFF's POA share seam. Least-privilege; fail closed (ADR-003).</role>

  <goal>For every secure root (project, matter, work assignment) the set of internal principals with access to each of its secure-team-owned children, and their rights, equals the root's POA share set and rights — at child create, after a share or unshare on the root through any path (BFF endpoints AND out-of-the-box MDA sharing), and after provisioning/unsecure — with the mechanism (platform cascade or BFF synchronizer) chosen from recorded live evidence, and never a child share wider than the root's.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>THE GAP. Today a secure root's children are owned in ordinary BUs, so a user shared on the secure project happens to read them by their ordinary role depth (which is also exactly the C10 leak). Task 146 fixes the leak by owning children with the named, memberless Secure team (task 144). From that moment the ONLY route to a child is a share on the child row itself: a sharee of the parent who is not shared on each child loses sight of every document, event, to-do and communication on the project they were invited to. That is why 146 is ship-together with this task.

WHO IS SHARED ON A SECURE ROOT, AND BY WHICH PATH (verified 2026-09-30):
  • provisioning Step 5.5 — creator + named colleagues: `Api/ExternalAccess/ProvisionProjectEndpoint.cs` :517-521 → `GrantAccessAsync` :726, :754 (generalized to matter/WA by task 144);
  • `/share-user` — `Api/ExternalAccess/InternalShareEndpoints.cs` route :148, grant :344, modify :346 (narrowed to the sharer's rights, `:261-311`);
  • `/unshare-user` — route :162, revoke :464;
  • `/unsecure-project` — revokes EVERY POA share on the root, `Api/ExternalAccess/UnsecureProjectEndpoint.cs` :373 (after the owner move at :168-226);
  • OUT-OF-THE-BOX MDA sharing — the Share privilege on project/matter/work assignment is held at DEEP by "Spaarke Core User" and "Spaarke Office Add In User" (session27 live facts), so a user holding ShareAccess on a secure root (the creator today; every Collaborate/Full sharee once task 139 lands — owner C4: "if the user has write access, then they can share (OOB functionality)") can share AND UNSHARE a root from the MDA without touching the BFF. Any BFF-only fan-out misses these — and an MDA UNSHARE that does not reach the children is an OVER-share: the removed user keeps every child until something repairs it.
  • Task 142 (Assigned-To auto-grants — a POA share for a linked systemuser) and task 143 (No Access List for internal users on secure records — refuses and REMOVES root shares, and its POML `143-no-access-internal-secure-shares.poml:246` says "task 149 must call this task's guard before any child-share fan-out and let the enforcer remove walled users' child shares") are further root-share writers; whichever of 142/143/149 lands second wires the fan-out and the guard.
The single POA client is `Services/Access/IDataverseRecordShareService.cs` (grant/modify/revoke + `GetPrincipalAccessOrThrowAsync` :63 — the read to use when a WRITE is decided from current shares); share masks in `Services/Access/RecordShareLevels.cs`. `PoaShareClientSingletonGuardTests` (ArchTests) forbids a second POA client.

THE PLATFORM OPTION. A Dataverse 1:N relationship carries a cascade configuration for Share, Unshare, Assign and Reparent. If root→child relationships cascade Share/Unshare, sharing the root shares its children automatically — including shares made in the MDA. What must be established LIVE, not assumed:
  (a) the current CascadeConfiguration of every root→child relationship (design.md §5.1d lists 19 lookups on sprk_project alone; sprk_matter and sprk_workassignment have their own sets);
  (b) whether a child CREATED after the root was shared becomes visible to the root's existing sharees (a share cascade acts at share time; Reparent-cascade inheritance may or may not cover create);
  (c) whether a child whose parent lookup is SET later (reparent) inherits;
  (d) whether unshare/modify cascade removes/narrows child access;
  (e) the platform limit on how many relationships of one child table may be parental/cascading — `sprk_document` has four root lookups (`sprk_project`, `sprk_relatedproject`, `sprk_matter`, `sprk_workassignment`), so a single cascading relationship may not cover all of them;
  (f) whether a cascade would also cascade ASSIGN (which would re-own children on provisioning/unsecure — an interaction with 144/148, see escalation);
  (g) whether a POA share on a child SURVIVES an Assign of that child to the Secure team (task 148's provisioning order mirrors shares before the re-own and depends on this answer);
  (h) that a relationship's cascade configuration is TABLE-WIDE: it applies to the children of NON-secure roots too, so enabling Share cascade changes today's behaviour for every ordinary project, matter and work assignment (see escalation).
The owner's test users (existing non-admin users in the correct BU) are the probe principals. Do not relocate users.

THE BFF OPTION (used for whatever the platform does not cover). One synchronizer, three triggers:
  • child create / reparent under a secure root (the owner hook task 146 installs) → copy the root's current share set (principal + rights) onto the child;
  • share / modify / unshare on the root through every BFF path above → apply the same delta to every secure-team-owned child of that root;
  • reconcile(root) → diff root share set vs each child's share set and repair (adds missing, removes extra, narrows wider). This is what catches OOB MDA shares; task 147 schedules it (ADR-036) and task 148 calls it at provisioning/unsecure/backfill.

SPA/TEAMS PLANE IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION. The evaluator's one-hop child inheritance (task 055, open) serves SPA/Teams; it does not give MDA/Office users Dataverse row access. This task is about Dataverse row access for internal users; it must not change the evaluator.</background>
